2026 08 21

OSNS_dnssd
----------


ONSN_dnssd user the DNS SD protocol to detect available services in the local network.
It uses the mdns implementation at:

https://github.com/mjansson/mdns

It sends discovery eand query request, and processes the respones, and stores them
in a backaend. This backend can be:

- ldap like OpenLDAP
- sql like SQLITE
- filesystem as fallback

At moment of writing the first backend ldap is being worked on.

The purpose is that appilactions can easily browse the network services,
well much easier than doing mdsn self (using ldap client library, sql client library or simple fs ops).

News

28 augustus 2026

IU dropped backend ldap and sqlite. The backend filsystem is so effectiev, ans powerfull,
and applications do not need an extra library, any application can reed filesystem directories and files.

The dnssd map looks like (for example)

%DNSSD ROOT% /
		host:00000001 -> host:00000001:examplehostA
		host:00000001:examplehostA
		    ipv4:00000001:192.168.0.1
		    srv:00000001:ssh
		    srv:00000002:cups
		host:00000001 -> host:00000001:examplehostB
		host:00000001:examplehostB
		    ipv4:00000002:192.168.0.2
		    srv:00000003:smb

DNS SD detected here in this example two hosts (examplehostA and examplehostB) with the following addresses and services
examplehostA (ipv4 192.168.0.1, sftp, cups)
examplehostB (ipv4 192.168.0.2, smb)

OSNS DNSSD tries to reckognize the service (by looking at /etc/services) and drops those which it cannot find.

OSNS DNSSD does only discovery at this moment.

Known issues: sending a term signal does not end the program. Working on it.


Remark about services announced by DNS SD
_________________________________________

It's common practice to announce printers managed by CUPS all via DNS SD. This is nonsense. The CUPS service (a printer broker/manager)
has to be announced, and only that. If clients want to get a list, they can use the CUPS API.

This also counts for the SSH protocol. SSH supports by protocol:
- exec a remote command/program/script
- start a shell
- start a subsystem (default supported sftp)

If the SSH supports more subsytems, it's not the task of DNS SD to provide those.
There is a problem here, since SSHH does noet have any other way to let clients know what susystems
it uses, other than a special extensions like

    supported@ssh.osns.net

which gives the client a list of susystemms it uses.


